1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,062
This is the complete walkthrough of your Exam Two Study Guide, including every derivative-table entry and every

2
00:00:06,062 --> 00:00:07,372
true or false statement.

3
00:00:07,642 --> 00:00:12,983
We will explain the rules, show the algebra, and include units in the applications.

4
00:00:13,253 --> 00:00:15,970
This is a study guide, rather than a new exam.

5
00:00:17,090 --> 00:00:19,772
Use the chapter markers to jump to a problem.

6
00:00:20,042 --> 00:00:25,347
The new narration uses a neural synthetic voice, with a slower pace and more consistent volume.

7
00:00:25,617 --> 00:00:28,311
Yellow highlights the current transformation.

8
00:00:28,581 --> 00:00:30,485
Captions follow the explanation.

9
00:00:30,755 --> 00:00:33,216
Pause before the next reveal to practice.

10
00:00:34,336 --> 00:00:36,426
For part a, use the power rule.

11
00:00:36,696 --> 00:00:41,444
Bring the exponent down as a multiplier, then subtract one from the exponent.

12
00:00:41,714 --> 00:00:44,002
X to the fourth becomes four x cubed.

13
00:00:45,122 --> 00:00:47,920
For b, e to the x is its own derivative.

14
00:00:48,190 --> 00:00:51,673
For c, rewrite the square root as x to the one half.

15
00:00:51,943 --> 00:00:58,494
The power rule gives one half times x to the negative one half, which is one over two square root x, for

16
00:00:58,494 --> 00:00:59,187
positive x.

17
00:01:00,307 --> 00:01:03,872
For d, one over x squared is x to the negative two.

18
00:01:04,142 --> 00:01:07,067
The power rule still works with a negative exponent.

19
00:01:07,337 --> 00:01:11,633
The derivative is negative two over x cubed, with x not zero.

20
00:01:12,753 --> 00:01:15,737
In part e, the base is two, not e.

21
00:01:16,007 --> 00:01:21,521
An exponential with constant positive base a differentiates to a to the x times natural log a.

22
00:01:21,791 --> 00:01:25,797
Do not use the ordinary power rule when x is in the exponent.

23
00:01:26,917 --> 00:01:30,202
Parts f and i both say sine x in the PDF.

24
00:01:30,472 --> 00:01:32,400
Their derivative is cosine x.

25
00:01:32,670 --> 00:01:35,375
We are keeping that repeated question as printed.

26
00:01:36,495 --> 00:01:39,119
Part g is one over x, for positive x.

27
00:01:39,389 --> 00:01:46,297
For h, use change of base: log base four of x equals natural log x divided by natural log four.

28
00:01:46,567 --> 00:01:49,225
That constant denominator stays in the derivative.

29
00:01:50,345 --> 00:01:54,885
Part j has a negative sign: cosine differentiates to negative sine.

30
00:01:55,155 --> 00:01:59,021
Part k, tangent, differentiates to secant squared.

31
00:01:59,291 --> 00:02:02,031
These formulas assume angles in radians.

32
00:02:03,151 --> 00:02:05,694
Part l is negative cosecant squared.

33
00:02:05,964 --> 00:02:08,158
Part m is secant times tangent.

34
00:02:08,428 --> 00:02:12,793
Evaluate these only where the original trigonometric function is defined.

35
00:02:13,913 --> 00:02:17,048
Part n is negative cosecant times cotangent.

36
00:02:17,318 --> 00:02:24,040
A useful memory check: the derivatives of cosine, cotangent, and cosecant have a leading minus sign.

37
00:02:25,160 --> 00:02:30,187
In part o, sine to the negative one means inverse sine, also called arc sine.

38
00:02:30,457 --> 00:02:32,756
It does not mean reciprocal sine.

39
00:02:33,026 --> 00:02:39,690
Its derivative is one over square root of one minus x squared, for x strictly between negative one and one.

40
00:02:40,810 --> 00:02:45,210
Inverse cosine has the same square root denominator and a negative sign.

41
00:02:45,480 --> 00:02:49,892
Its derivative also requires negative one less than x less than one.

42
00:02:51,012 --> 00:02:54,843
Inverse tangent differentiates to one over one plus x squared.

43
00:02:55,113 --> 00:03:01,000
Unlike inverse sine and inverse cosine, this derivative is defined for every real x.

44
00:03:02,120 --> 00:03:06,055
Parts r and s are hyperbolic sine and hyperbolic cosine.

45
00:03:06,325 --> 00:03:12,630
They differentiate into each other, and neither formula has the negative sign that appears for ordinary cosine.

46
00:03:13,750 --> 00:03:20,518
The derivative of hyperbolic tangent is hyperbolic secant squared, meaning one over hyperbolic cosine squared.

47
00:03:20,788 --> 00:03:24,457
Keep the hyperbolic functions distinct from ordinary trigonometry.

48
00:03:25,577 --> 00:03:29,698
For part u, one over the cube root of x is x to the negative one third.

49
00:03:29,968 --> 00:03:35,216
Multiply by negative one third, then reduce the exponent to negative four thirds.

50
00:03:35,486 --> 00:03:40,084
This real cube-root formula works on either side of zero, but not at zero.

51
00:03:41,204 --> 00:03:43,444
Problem two asks for two derivatives.

52
00:03:43,714 --> 00:03:47,314
Apply the sum rule, differentiating each term separately.

53
00:03:47,584 --> 00:03:51,171
A constant has zero derivative because it does not change.

54
00:03:52,291 --> 00:03:59,002
X squared becomes two x, negative ten x becomes negative ten, and one hundred becomes zero.

55
00:03:59,272 --> 00:04:04,148
Differentiate again: two x becomes two and negative ten becomes zero.

56
00:04:05,268 --> 00:04:09,447
For problem three, use the sine and cosine formulas separately.

57
00:04:09,717 --> 00:04:12,852
Watch the sign when cosine is differentiated.

58
00:04:13,972 --> 00:04:16,631
The first derivative is cosine minus sine.

59
00:04:16,901 --> 00:04:20,395
Differentiating again gives negative sine minus cosine.

60
00:04:20,665 --> 00:04:24,148
The minus before sine stays when taking its derivative.

61
00:04:25,268 --> 00:04:28,310
Problem four is a cube whose inside is sixteen x.

62
00:04:28,580 --> 00:04:31,796
We can use the chain rule or expand the constant first.

63
00:04:32,066 --> 00:04:34,469
We will show both ways to check the result.

64
00:04:35,589 --> 00:04:41,151
The chain rule gives three times the inside squared, times the inside derivative sixteen.

65
00:04:41,421 --> 00:04:48,340
Alternatively, sixteen cubed is four thousand ninety six, so the original is four thousand ninety six x cubed.

66
00:04:48,610 --> 00:04:52,349
Both methods give twelve thousand two hundred eighty eight x squared.

67
00:04:53,469 --> 00:04:57,323
For problem five, keep the constant multiplier negative two.

68
00:04:57,593 --> 00:05:01,796
Cosine becomes negative sine, and tangent becomes secant squared.

69
00:05:02,916 --> 00:05:04,541
This is the first derivative.

70
00:05:04,811 --> 00:05:10,581
To differentiate secant squared, view it as the square of secant x and use the chain rule.

71
00:05:11,701 --> 00:05:16,531
The derivative of secant squared is two secant x times secant x tangent x.

72
00:05:16,801 --> 00:05:21,782
Multiplying by negative two gives negative four secant squared x tangent x.

73
00:05:22,902 --> 00:05:25,421
Rewrite the reciprocal as a negative power.

74
00:05:25,691 --> 00:05:29,279
This avoids using the quotient rule for a simple reciprocal.

75
00:05:30,399 --> 00:05:33,313
The first derivative uses the power rule twice.

76
00:05:33,583 --> 00:05:37,530
Negative exponents obey the same bring-down-and-subtract-one rule.

77
00:05:38,650 --> 00:05:40,067
Differentiate again.

78
00:05:40,337 --> 00:05:46,026
Negative two times negative three gives positive six, and the exponent becomes negative four.

79
00:05:47,146 --> 00:05:48,864
Problem seven is a quotient.

80
00:05:49,134 --> 00:05:53,499
Here both the numerator and denominator change, so use the quotient rule.

81
00:05:54,619 --> 00:05:58,044
Multiply the denominator by the derivative of the numerator.

82
00:05:58,314 --> 00:06:02,946
Subtract the numerator times the derivative of the denominator, which is one.

83
00:06:03,216 --> 00:06:05,887
Divide by the original denominator squared.

84
00:06:06,157 --> 00:06:09,106
Keep x nonzero and cosine x nonzero.

85
00:06:10,226 --> 00:06:13,511
For fractional exponents, the power rule still applies.

86
00:06:13,781 --> 00:06:16,765
Subtract one by writing one as three thirds.

87
00:06:17,885 --> 00:06:20,010
Four thirds minus one is one third.

88
00:06:20,280 --> 00:06:22,985
Two thirds minus one is negative one third.

89
00:06:23,255 --> 00:06:28,479
At zero, this derivative is not finite; the original function has a cusp there.

90
00:06:29,599 --> 00:06:31,143
Problem nine is a quotient.

91
00:06:31,413 --> 00:06:34,386
The denominator changes, so we need the quotient rule.

92
00:06:34,656 --> 00:06:39,114
The original function is defined only where one plus cosine x is not zero.

93
00:06:40,234 --> 00:06:46,503
Use bottom times derivative of top, minus top times derivative of bottom, over bottom squared.

94
00:06:46,773 --> 00:06:49,536
The derivative of cosine is negative sine.

95
00:06:49,806 --> 00:06:52,639
Notice the two minus signs in the second product.

96
00:06:53,759 --> 00:06:55,895
The double negative becomes a plus.

97
00:06:56,165 --> 00:06:59,846
Now use sine squared plus cosine squared equals one.

98
00:07:00,116 --> 00:07:02,914
This identity simplifies the numerator.

99
00:07:04,034 --> 00:07:06,890
Cancel one factor of one plus cosine x.

100
00:07:07,160 --> 00:07:09,644
Keep the restriction from the original function.

101
00:07:09,914 --> 00:07:13,792
The most common error here is losing the minus sign in the quotient rule.

102
00:07:14,912 --> 00:07:17,954
Problem ten is a product of two changing factors.

103
00:07:18,224 --> 00:07:20,801
We cannot simply multiply their derivatives.

104
00:07:21,071 --> 00:07:24,380
Name the first factor u and the second factor v.

105
00:07:25,500 --> 00:07:29,958
The product rule is u prime times v, plus u times v prime.

106
00:07:30,228 --> 00:07:34,536
Think: change one factor at a time, while keeping the other factor.

107
00:07:35,656 --> 00:07:38,105
The derivative of the first factor is two.

108
00:07:38,375 --> 00:07:45,783
For the logarithm, use the chain rule: the derivative of the inside, six x, divided by the original inside.

109
00:07:46,903 --> 00:07:49,016
Substitute into the product rule.

110
00:07:49,286 --> 00:07:51,190
Both terms are necessary.

111
00:07:51,460 --> 00:07:56,673
You may leave the derivative in this factored form; expanding it does not make it more correct.

112
00:07:57,793 --> 00:08:01,508
Problem eleven combines the product rule with two chain rules.

113
00:08:01,778 --> 00:08:06,758
The superscript negative one means inverse tangent, not reciprocal tangent.

114
00:08:07,878 --> 00:08:10,851
The exponential needs the inside derivative two.

115
00:08:11,121 --> 00:08:17,843
Inverse tangent gives one over one plus its inside squared, multiplied by the inside derivative one half.

116
00:08:18,113 --> 00:08:21,120
Simplifying gives two over x squared plus four.

117
00:08:22,240 --> 00:08:25,444
Use the product rule and factor out e to the two x.

118
00:08:25,714 --> 00:08:29,696
There are two terms inside the brackets because either factor can change.

119
00:08:30,816 --> 00:08:35,054
Use the inverse sine derivative, not the ordinary sine derivative.

120
00:08:35,324 --> 00:08:37,867
Name the inside x squared minus one.

121
00:08:38,987 --> 00:08:42,028
Put the inside derivative two x in the numerator.

122
00:08:42,298 --> 00:08:46,443
The denominator is square root of one minus the entire inside squared.

123
00:08:46,713 --> 00:08:49,743
The formula applies where the denominator is positive.

124
00:08:50,863 --> 00:08:55,925
The original inverse sine is real when absolute x is at most square root two.

125
00:08:56,195 --> 00:09:01,106
The derivative formula requires the inside strictly between negative one and one.

126
00:09:01,376 --> 00:09:05,835
Zero is excluded: the function has different one-sided slopes there.

127
00:09:06,955 --> 00:09:10,774
Problem thirteen uses theta as the independent variable.

128
00:09:11,044 --> 00:09:13,598
The rules are identical to the rules for x.

129
00:09:13,868 --> 00:09:16,481
Treat the sum in parentheses as one factor.

130
00:09:17,601 --> 00:09:21,966
Differentiate tangent for the first term and the parenthesized sum for the second.

131
00:09:22,236 --> 00:09:25,963
The derivative of the sum is cosine theta minus sine theta.

132
00:09:27,083 --> 00:09:31,599
For problem fourteen, divide the numerator into x minus one plus three.

133
00:09:31,869 --> 00:09:36,188
This rewriting makes both derivatives easier and keeps x not equal to one.

134
00:09:37,308 --> 00:09:39,293
One differentiates to zero.

135
00:09:39,563 --> 00:09:45,612
Three times x minus one to the negative one becomes negative three times x minus one to the negative two.

136
00:09:46,732 --> 00:09:48,149
Differentiate again.

137
00:09:48,419 --> 00:09:51,275
Negative three times negative two gives six.

138
00:09:51,545 --> 00:09:55,492
The inside derivative is one, so it adds no extra factor.

139
00:09:56,612 --> 00:09:58,121
Use the product rule.

140
00:09:58,391 --> 00:10:02,048
Both cosecant and cotangent have negative derivatives.

141
00:10:02,318 --> 00:10:05,047
This problem is a useful test of sign tracking.

142
00:10:06,167 --> 00:10:10,590
The first product contributes negative cosecant times cotangent squared.

143
00:10:10,860 --> 00:10:13,693
The second contributes negative cosecant cubed.

144
00:10:14,813 --> 00:10:18,865
For the second derivative, apply the product rule again to the first term.

145
00:10:19,135 --> 00:10:25,195
Differentiating cotangent squared uses the chain rule and contributes a second positive term.

146
00:10:26,315 --> 00:10:31,946
The derivative of negative cosecant cubed contributes another three cosecant cubed cotangent.

147
00:10:32,216 --> 00:10:35,130
Combine that with the two already present to get five.

148
00:10:36,250 --> 00:10:38,445
Differentiate with respect to u.

149
00:10:38,715 --> 00:10:43,695
The top derivative is negative two u, and the bottom derivative is positive two u.

150
00:10:44,815 --> 00:10:47,776
The quotient rule subtracts the second product.

151
00:10:48,046 --> 00:10:51,749
Keep the parentheses so the minus sign applies to the entire product.

152
00:10:52,869 --> 00:10:56,004
Expanding the numerator cancels the cubic terms.

153
00:10:56,274 --> 00:10:58,782
The two linear terms add to negative four u.

154
00:10:59,052 --> 00:11:01,722
The denominator never vanishes for real u.

155
00:11:02,842 --> 00:11:05,303
Rewrite the square root as a one-half power.

156
00:11:05,573 --> 00:11:10,519
The outer derivative must be multiplied by the derivative of the polynomial inside.

157
00:11:11,639 --> 00:11:14,426
The inside derivative is two x minus seven.

158
00:11:14,696 --> 00:11:21,952
The formula applies when x squared minus seven x is positive, meaning x less than zero or x greater than seven.

159
00:11:22,222 --> 00:11:25,519
The endpoints do not have a finite two-sided derivative.

160
00:11:26,639 --> 00:11:29,518
Multiply powers of x by adding exponents.

161
00:11:29,788 --> 00:11:36,162
The first term has exponent one plus one half, and the reciprocal term has exponent negative two minus one half.

162
00:11:36,432 --> 00:11:38,464
The real domain is x positive.

163
00:11:39,584 --> 00:11:41,743
Use the power rule on each term.

164
00:11:42,013 --> 00:11:47,052
This is simpler than using both a product rule and a quotient rule on the original expression.

165
00:11:48,172 --> 00:11:53,385
Identify the outside power, three halves, and the inside t minus one over t.

166
00:11:53,655 --> 00:11:56,279
The chain rule multiplies their derivatives.

167
00:11:57,399 --> 00:12:01,869
The derivative of negative one over t is positive one over t squared.

168
00:12:02,139 --> 00:12:03,555
Keep that plus sign.

169
00:12:03,825 --> 00:12:08,771
This formula is used on the interior of the real domain where the inside is positive.

170
00:12:09,891 --> 00:12:12,503
The whole polynomial is raised to the fourth power.

171
00:12:12,773 --> 00:12:15,420
Keep it together while taking the outer derivative.

172
00:12:16,540 --> 00:12:19,687
Bring down four and lower the outer exponent to three.

173
00:12:19,957 --> 00:12:24,752
Then multiply by three x squared minus five, the derivative of the inside.

174
00:12:25,872 --> 00:12:29,482
This is a product, and each factor also needs the chain rule.

175
00:12:29,752 --> 00:12:32,771
Write two product-rule terms before simplifying.

176
00:12:33,891 --> 00:12:38,094
The first factor differentiates to three times its square times twelve t.

177
00:12:38,364 --> 00:12:42,439
The second differentiates to four times its cube times three t squared.

178
00:12:42,709 --> 00:12:44,973
Keep the untouched factor in each term.

179
00:12:46,093 --> 00:12:47,974
This factored form is optional.

180
00:12:48,244 --> 00:12:50,867
Pull out the factors shared by both terms.

181
00:12:51,137 --> 00:12:55,375
The unsimplified two-term version is also a complete correct derivative.

182
00:12:56,495 --> 00:12:58,887
The independent variable is y here.

183
00:12:59,157 --> 00:13:03,615
We need the chain rule for the outer cube and the quotient rule for the inside fraction.

184
00:13:04,735 --> 00:13:10,958
The numerator simplifies to thirteen because y cancels and subtracting negative six adds six.

185
00:13:12,078 --> 00:13:16,211
Multiply the derivative of the outer cube by the derivative of the inside.

186
00:13:16,481 --> 00:13:19,418
Combining the denominator powers gives a fourth power.

187
00:13:19,688 --> 00:13:21,592
Y cannot be negative seven.

188
00:13:22,712 --> 00:13:28,308
There are multiple layers here: a square root outside, and two exponentials inside.

189
00:13:28,578 --> 00:13:31,527
Differentiate the entire expression under the radical.

190
00:13:32,647 --> 00:13:34,842
The first exponent contributes four.

191
00:13:35,112 --> 00:13:37,689
The second exponent contributes two x.

192
00:13:37,959 --> 00:13:40,200
Keep the subtraction between the terms.

193
00:13:41,320 --> 00:13:45,232
Divide the inside derivative by twice the original square root.

194
00:13:45,502 --> 00:13:50,274
For a finite derivative this requires the expression under the radical to be positive.

195
00:13:50,544 --> 00:13:53,528
Here that means zero less than x less than four.

196
00:13:54,648 --> 00:13:57,434
The powers apply to the trigonometric values.

197
00:13:57,704 --> 00:14:00,990
Sine cubed x means sine x, all cubed.

198
00:14:01,260 --> 00:14:03,640
Differentiate each cube with the chain rule.

199
00:14:04,760 --> 00:14:08,336
The sine term contributes cosine as its inside derivative.

200
00:14:08,606 --> 00:14:12,971
The cosine term contributes negative sine, which creates the minus sign.

201
00:14:14,091 --> 00:14:17,969
Factor out three sine x cosine x if you want a compact answer.

202
00:14:18,239 --> 00:14:20,839
Both forms describe the same derivative.

203
00:14:21,959 --> 00:14:26,244
Problem twenty five means the square of sine of cosine of k x.

204
00:14:26,514 --> 00:14:27,872
Treat k as a constant.

205
00:14:28,142 --> 00:14:30,499
Work from the outside toward the inside.

206
00:14:31,619 --> 00:14:33,709
The outermost operation is squaring.

207
00:14:33,979 --> 00:14:40,109
Its derivative is two times the unchanged sine, multiplied by the derivative of that sine.

208
00:14:41,229 --> 00:14:44,909
The derivative of sine u is cosine u times u prime.

209
00:14:45,179 --> 00:14:48,697
We still owe a derivative for the cosine layer and for k x.

210
00:14:49,817 --> 00:14:52,394
Cosine differentiates to negative sine.

211
00:14:52,664 --> 00:14:55,520
Then the inside k x differentiates to k.

212
00:14:55,790 --> 00:14:58,553
Every changing layer contributes one factor.

213
00:14:59,673 --> 00:15:01,403
Put the layers back together.

214
00:15:01,673 --> 00:15:05,354
Read the factors from outside to inside to check that none is missing.

215
00:15:05,624 --> 00:15:09,455
In particular, remember both the negative sign and the factor k.

216
00:15:10,575 --> 00:15:14,708
Use the product rule for x times hyperbolic sine of x squared.

217
00:15:14,978 --> 00:15:20,365
Hyperbolic sine differentiates to hyperbolic cosine, with an additional inside derivative.

218
00:15:21,485 --> 00:15:23,366
The derivative of x is one.

219
00:15:23,636 --> 00:15:29,220
In the second term, keep x and multiply hyperbolic cosine of x squared by two x.

220
00:15:30,340 --> 00:15:31,943
Combine the x factors.

221
00:15:32,213 --> 00:15:36,172
There is no negative sign when differentiating hyperbolic sine.

222
00:15:37,292 --> 00:15:42,667
For a logarithm of a function, differentiate the inside and divide by the original inside.

223
00:15:42,937 --> 00:15:46,791
Then use the chain rule again for hyperbolic cosine of three x.

224
00:15:47,911 --> 00:15:53,287
Hyperbolic cosine differentiates to hyperbolic sine, and three x contributes three.

225
00:15:53,557 --> 00:15:57,713
Hyperbolic sine divided by hyperbolic cosine is hyperbolic tangent.

226
00:15:58,833 --> 00:16:00,552
Treat y as a function of x.

227
00:16:00,822 --> 00:16:07,161
The middle term is a product of x squared and y squared, so it needs a product rule as well as a chain rule.

228
00:16:08,281 --> 00:16:11,636
The derivative of y to the fifth includes y prime.

229
00:16:11,906 --> 00:16:17,258
In the middle term, differentiate x squared for one term and y squared for the other.

230
00:16:17,528 --> 00:16:20,256
The constant twelve differentiates to zero.

231
00:16:21,376 --> 00:16:24,558
Collect the terms containing y prime on one side.

232
00:16:24,828 --> 00:16:29,170
Factor out y prime, then move the remaining terms to the other side.

233
00:16:30,290 --> 00:16:34,516
Divide by the coefficient of y prime, wherever it is nonzero.

234
00:16:34,786 --> 00:16:40,254
This gives the slope along the implicit curve at points where that expression defines a derivative.

235
00:16:41,374 --> 00:16:43,673
Here y is an unknown function of x.

236
00:16:43,943 --> 00:16:50,637
Differentiate both sides with respect to x, and attach y prime whenever the chain rule differentiates a y

237
00:16:50,637 --> 00:16:51,338
expression.

238
00:16:52,458 --> 00:16:56,754
The derivative of y to the fourth is four y cubed times y prime.

239
00:16:57,024 --> 00:16:58,348
Solve for y prime.

240
00:16:58,618 --> 00:17:05,225
This formula requires y to be nonzero; it describes the slope where the curve is locally a differentiable

241
00:17:05,225 --> 00:17:06,106
function of x.

242
00:17:07,226 --> 00:17:13,019
For the second derivative, write y prime as negative x cubed times y to the negative third power.

243
00:17:13,289 --> 00:17:17,573
Apply the product rule, and again use the chain rule on the y factor.

244
00:17:18,693 --> 00:17:22,560
Substitute negative x cubed over y cubed for y prime.

245
00:17:22,830 --> 00:17:24,803
The second term becomes negative.

246
00:17:25,073 --> 00:17:29,427
A second derivative should not still contain an unexplained y prime here.

247
00:17:30,547 --> 00:17:31,859
Combine the fractions.

248
00:17:32,129 --> 00:17:38,503
The original equation says x to the fourth plus y to the fourth is sixteen, so the numerator simplifies.

249
00:17:38,773 --> 00:17:42,999
Reusing the original relationship often makes implicit answers much cleaner.

250
00:17:44,119 --> 00:17:46,476
We could use an implicit quotient rule.

251
00:17:46,746 --> 00:17:50,740
A simpler option is to multiply by the nonzero denominator first.

252
00:17:51,010 --> 00:17:54,794
Keep its restriction because multiplying can hide an excluded point.

253
00:17:55,914 --> 00:18:00,431
Expand the right side, move its y term to the left, and factor y.

254
00:18:00,701 --> 00:18:02,825
Now we can solve explicitly.

255
00:18:03,945 --> 00:18:08,195
The new denominator x squared plus two never vanishes for real x.

256
00:18:08,465 --> 00:18:15,630
But the original denominator equals x over x squared plus two, so the original equation excludes x equal to

257
00:18:15,630 --> 00:18:15,965
zero.

258
00:18:17,085 --> 00:18:20,254
Differentiate the explicit quotient and simplify.

259
00:18:20,524 --> 00:18:26,735
The original curve still excludes zero, even though the simplified derivative expression has a value there.

260
00:18:27,855 --> 00:18:29,550
Both terms are products.

261
00:18:29,820 --> 00:18:34,000
Cosine y also needs the chain rule because y depends on x.

262
00:18:35,120 --> 00:18:38,522
Differentiate x cosine y with the product rule.

263
00:18:38,792 --> 00:18:42,286
Differentiate y cosine x with another product rule.

264
00:18:42,556 --> 00:18:46,074
Notice which derivatives create y prime and which do not.

265
00:18:47,194 --> 00:18:48,959
Collect y prime terms.

266
00:18:49,229 --> 00:18:53,095
Move the other terms to the opposite side, keeping both signs.

267
00:18:54,215 --> 00:18:57,675
Divide by the remaining coefficient where it is nonzero.

268
00:18:57,945 --> 00:19:04,528
Keeping the implicit variables in the answer is expected; there is no need to solve the original equation for y.

269
00:19:05,648 --> 00:19:11,789
Both the base and exponent depend on x, so the ordinary constant exponent power rule does not apply.

270
00:19:12,059 --> 00:19:18,120
We work where the base is positive, giving a real logarithm and the usual smooth variable power.

271
00:19:19,240 --> 00:19:21,980
Take natural logarithms of both sides.

272
00:19:22,250 --> 00:19:24,386
The exponent comes down in front.

273
00:19:24,656 --> 00:19:29,428
This turns a difficult variable power into a product that we know how to differentiate.

274
00:19:30,548 --> 00:19:33,322
The left side becomes y prime over y.

275
00:19:33,592 --> 00:19:35,833
On the right, use the product rule.

276
00:19:36,103 --> 00:19:41,595
The logarithm also needs the chain rule, producing six x squared over the inside.

277
00:19:42,715 --> 00:19:46,476
Multiply by y, then replace y with the original function.

278
00:19:46,746 --> 00:19:49,881
Forgetting this final multiplication is a common mistake.

279
00:19:50,151 --> 00:19:55,445
Logarithmic differentiation is especially useful when a variable appears in the exponent.

280
00:19:56,565 --> 00:20:01,418
Both base and exponent vary, so use logarithmic differentiation again.

281
00:20:01,688 --> 00:20:05,752
Work where tangent x is positive so the real logarithm is defined.

282
00:20:06,872 --> 00:20:10,111
Take a logarithm and bring the sine x exponent in front.

283
00:20:10,381 --> 00:20:12,413
The right side is now a product.

284
00:20:13,533 --> 00:20:15,692
Differentiate with the product rule.

285
00:20:15,962 --> 00:20:20,258
The logarithm of tangent has derivative secant squared divided by tangent.

286
00:20:21,378 --> 00:20:23,351
Multiply by the original y.

287
00:20:23,621 --> 00:20:30,926
The second term inside the brackets simplifies to secant x because sine x times secant squared x divided by

288
00:20:30,926 --> 00:20:33,316
tangent x equals one over cosine x.

289
00:20:34,436 --> 00:20:37,199
A tangent line needs the point and its slope.

290
00:20:37,469 --> 00:20:41,997
Check the point: six divided by three is two, so it lies on the graph.

291
00:20:43,117 --> 00:20:47,563
Differentiate with the quotient rule, then evaluate at x equal to six.

292
00:20:47,833 --> 00:20:50,016
The derivative gives the tangent slope.

293
00:20:51,136 --> 00:20:57,905
Use point-slope form, y minus the given y coordinate equals the slope times x minus the given x coordinate.

294
00:20:58,175 --> 00:21:01,437
This form is already a complete tangent-line equation.

295
00:21:02,557 --> 00:21:07,724
At pi over six, sine is one half, so twice sine equals the given height one.

296
00:21:08,844 --> 00:21:11,073
The derivative of sine is cosine.

297
00:21:11,343 --> 00:21:17,647
Cosine pi over six is square root three over two, so multiplying by two gives square root three.

298
00:21:18,767 --> 00:21:21,925
Substitute the point and slope into point-slope form.

299
00:21:22,195 --> 00:21:24,958
Keep pi over six exact instead of rounding it.

300
00:21:26,078 --> 00:21:28,493
The angle in the cosine term is two x.

301
00:21:28,763 --> 00:21:32,745
That inside factor will contribute a two when we differentiate.

302
00:21:33,865 --> 00:21:35,862
Differentiate sine normally.

303
00:21:36,132 --> 00:21:41,298
Cosine two x becomes negative sine two x, times the inside derivative two.

304
00:21:42,418 --> 00:21:46,911
At x equal to pi over six, the doubled angle is pi over three.

305
00:21:47,181 --> 00:21:50,897
Both trigonometric values here are square root three over two.

306
00:21:52,017 --> 00:21:54,629
Use the negative slope with the given point.

307
00:21:54,899 --> 00:21:57,697
Do not forget the factor two from the doubled angle.

308
00:21:58,817 --> 00:22:01,870
Rewrite the quotient as a product with a negative exponent.

309
00:22:02,140 --> 00:22:05,960
This lets the product and chain rules handle the derivative cleanly.

310
00:22:07,080 --> 00:22:14,220
The derivative of the second factor is negative five times the inside to the negative six, times negative two x.

311
00:22:14,490 --> 00:22:16,510
The two negative signs cancel.

312
00:22:17,630 --> 00:22:19,314
Use a common denominator.

313
00:22:19,584 --> 00:22:24,588
Three minus x squared plus ten x squared becomes three plus nine x squared.

314
00:22:24,858 --> 00:22:29,444
At x equal to two, the denominator is one and the numerator is thirty nine.

315
00:22:30,564 --> 00:22:33,675
The tangent line is steep and increasing at this point.

316
00:22:33,945 --> 00:22:38,055
Use y plus two because the given y coordinate is negative two.

317
00:22:39,175 --> 00:22:43,703
This curve is implicit, so find the slope with implicit differentiation.

318
00:22:43,973 --> 00:22:49,453
The point satisfies the equation: twenty five sixteenths minus nine sixteenths equals one.

319
00:22:50,573 --> 00:22:52,697
Differentiate both squares.

320
00:22:52,967 --> 00:22:55,127
The y square needs y prime.

321
00:22:55,397 --> 00:22:58,346
Solve the resulting linear equation for y prime.

322
00:22:59,466 --> 00:23:02,542
Evaluate both x and y at the given point.

323
00:23:02,812 --> 00:23:06,423
An implicit derivative usually depends on both coordinates.

324
00:23:07,543 --> 00:23:10,829
Substitute the point and slope into point-slope form.

325
00:23:11,099 --> 00:23:15,522
The x expression is x plus five because the x coordinate is negative five.

326
00:23:16,642 --> 00:23:20,706
We need a product rule on the right and an implicit chain rule on the left.

327
00:23:21,826 --> 00:23:26,307
The first right-hand term differentiates x cubed and keeps two minus x.

328
00:23:26,577 --> 00:23:30,037
The second keeps x cubed and multiplies by negative one.

329
00:23:31,157 --> 00:23:33,456
At one comma one, the slope is one.

330
00:23:33,726 --> 00:23:39,716
Point-slope form gives y minus one equals x minus one, so the tangent is y equals x.

331
00:23:40,836 --> 00:23:43,205
Position tells us where the particle is.

332
00:23:43,475 --> 00:23:46,157
Velocity is the derivative of position.

333
00:23:46,427 --> 00:23:49,202
Acceleration is the derivative of velocity.

334
00:23:49,472 --> 00:23:52,502
Speed is the absolute value of velocity.

335
00:23:53,622 --> 00:23:56,408
Differentiate position and factor the result.

336
00:23:56,678 --> 00:24:02,576
The particle is at rest when velocity is zero, so the rest times are one second and two seconds.

337
00:24:03,696 --> 00:24:05,414
Differentiate velocity.

338
00:24:05,684 --> 00:24:08,784
Acceleration is zero at one point five seconds.

339
00:24:09,054 --> 00:24:14,894
That does not mean the particle is at rest: its velocity there is negative one point five feet per second.

340
00:24:16,014 --> 00:24:23,448
To determine speeding up, compare the signs of velocity and acceleration on intervals separated by one, one

341
00:24:23,448 --> 00:24:24,838
point five, and two.

342
00:24:25,108 --> 00:24:28,022
Do not decide from the sign of acceleration alone.

343
00:24:29,142 --> 00:24:33,089
Velocity has signs positive, negative, negative, positive.

344
00:24:33,359 --> 00:24:37,597
Acceleration has signs negative, negative, positive, positive.

345
00:24:37,867 --> 00:24:41,861
Matching signs increase speed; opposite signs decrease speed.

346
00:24:42,981 --> 00:24:48,077
The particle speeds up between one and one point five seconds, and after two seconds.

347
00:24:48,347 --> 00:24:53,247
It slows down between zero and one, and between one point five and two.

348
00:24:53,517 --> 00:24:57,511
These are open intervals; transition instants are handled separately.

349
00:24:58,631 --> 00:25:02,949
Problem forty one asks the same five motion questions as problem forty.

350
00:25:03,219 --> 00:25:06,261
Start with velocity, the derivative of position.

351
00:25:07,381 --> 00:25:09,866
Factor the velocity to find the rest times.

352
00:25:10,136 --> 00:25:13,375
Velocity is zero at one half second and one second.

353
00:25:13,645 --> 00:25:15,746
Its units are feet per second.

354
00:25:16,866 --> 00:25:21,162
Differentiate velocity to find acceleration in feet per second squared.

355
00:25:21,432 --> 00:25:24,091
It changes sign at three quarters of a second.

356
00:25:25,211 --> 00:25:29,414
Partition the allowed time line at one half, three quarters, and one.

357
00:25:29,684 --> 00:25:33,573
Test the signs of velocity and acceleration in each open interval.

358
00:25:34,693 --> 00:25:39,325
As in problem forty, the signs are opposite, matching, opposite, matching.

359
00:25:39,595 --> 00:25:45,377
A negative velocity with a negative acceleration means the particle is speeding up in the negative direction.

360
00:25:46,497 --> 00:25:51,710
The particle slows down before one half second and between three quarters and one second.

361
00:25:51,980 --> 00:25:56,252
It speeds up between one half and three quarters, and after one second.

362
00:25:56,522 --> 00:25:59,181
This completes all five requested parts.

363
00:26:00,301 --> 00:26:03,099
The cylinder radius is fixed at five meters.

364
00:26:03,369 --> 00:26:07,862
This is why we can replace pi r squared with the constant twenty five pi.

365
00:26:08,982 --> 00:26:11,223
Differentiate with respect to time.

366
00:26:11,493 --> 00:26:12,967
Only h changes.

367
00:26:13,237 --> 00:26:17,104
There is no radius-rate term because the radius is constant.

368
00:26:18,224 --> 00:26:21,335
Substitute the incoming volume rate and divide.

369
00:26:21,605 --> 00:26:25,947
The water height rises at about zero point zero three eight two meters per minute.

370
00:26:27,067 --> 00:26:28,751
Let a be the changing edge length.

371
00:26:29,021 --> 00:26:34,199
Both volume and surface area depend on a, so use a as the bridge between their rates.

372
00:26:35,319 --> 00:26:39,022
Differentiate the volume and use edge length thirty centimeters.

373
00:26:39,292 --> 00:26:43,123
This gives the edge-length rate, not the requested area rate yet.

374
00:26:44,243 --> 00:26:48,052
Differentiate surface area and substitute the edge-length rate.

375
00:26:48,322 --> 00:26:51,642
The final answer is four thirds square centimeters per minute.

376
00:26:52,762 --> 00:26:54,794
Measure t in hours after noon.

377
00:26:55,064 --> 00:26:59,383
Let x be the east west separation and z the north south separation.

378
00:26:59,653 --> 00:27:06,677
Before the ships pass east west, x decreases at thirty five kilometers per hour, while z increases at twenty

379
00:27:06,677 --> 00:27:07,002
five.

380
00:27:08,122 --> 00:27:11,187
The distance is the hypotenuse of a right triangle.

381
00:27:11,457 --> 00:27:16,925
Differentiate the squared distance equation with respect to time before substituting the time.

382
00:27:17,195 --> 00:27:19,831
Every changing quantity contributes its rate.

383
00:27:20,951 --> 00:27:23,563
At four p m, four hours have passed.

384
00:27:23,833 --> 00:27:28,976
The horizontal separation is ten kilometers, and the vertical separation is one hundred.

385
00:27:29,246 --> 00:27:32,404
Use these current values, not the noon separation.

386
00:27:33,524 --> 00:27:37,042
Divide by D and substitute both rates with their signs.

387
00:27:37,312 --> 00:27:43,610
The result is positive, about twenty one point three nine kilometers per hour, so the ships are moving farther

388
00:27:43,610 --> 00:27:44,870
apart at that instant.

389
00:27:45,990 --> 00:27:49,589
The cone has total radius two meters and height four meters.

390
00:27:49,859 --> 00:27:52,634
Water enters at two cubic meters per minute.

391
00:27:52,904 --> 00:27:57,304
Unlike a cylinder, the water surface radius changes as the water rises.

392
00:27:58,424 --> 00:28:02,813
Use similar triangles to replace the changing radius with h over two.

393
00:28:03,083 --> 00:28:07,285
Substitute this relationship into the volume formula before differentiating.

394
00:28:07,555 --> 00:28:10,806
Now volume depends on just one unknown changing length.

395
00:28:11,926 --> 00:28:14,190
Differentiate with respect to time.

396
00:28:14,460 --> 00:28:19,232
The derivative of h cubed is three h squared times d h over d t.

397
00:28:19,502 --> 00:28:22,172
The factor of three reduces twelve to four.

398
00:28:23,292 --> 00:28:26,682
Now substitute a water depth of three meters and solve.

399
00:28:26,952 --> 00:28:30,714
The height rises at about zero point two eight three meters per minute.

400
00:28:30,984 --> 00:28:35,872
Check the units: a height rate uses meters per minute, not cubic meters per minute.

401
00:28:36,992 --> 00:28:40,614
Let x be distance along the path from the point nearest the light.

402
00:28:40,884 --> 00:28:43,914
Let theta be the angle from the perpendicular to the path.

403
00:28:44,184 --> 00:28:49,339
The perpendicular distance is twenty feet, so tangent theta equals x over twenty.

404
00:28:49,609 --> 00:28:52,419
We take x increasing as the man walks away.

405
00:28:53,539 --> 00:28:55,849
Differentiate with respect to time.

406
00:28:56,119 --> 00:29:01,320
The angle changes, so the derivative of tangent includes d theta over d t.

407
00:29:02,440 --> 00:29:06,202
At the requested position, tangent theta is three quarters.

408
00:29:06,472 --> 00:29:13,310
The identity secant squared equals one plus tangent squared avoids needing to calculate the angle itself.

409
00:29:14,430 --> 00:29:19,237
Divide by secant squared to obtain zero point one two eight radians per second.

410
00:29:19,507 --> 00:29:21,724
This is the rotation-rate magnitude.

411
00:29:21,994 --> 00:29:27,126
If the man were approaching the nearest point, the signed angle rate would be negative instead.

412
00:29:28,246 --> 00:29:30,010
The building height is constant.

413
00:29:30,280 --> 00:29:35,238
The sun angle is decreasing, so its signed rate is negative one quarter radian per hour.

414
00:29:35,508 --> 00:29:37,122
Let L be the shadow length.

415
00:29:38,242 --> 00:29:42,317
Use the right triangle: opposite over adjacent equals tangent.

416
00:29:42,587 --> 00:29:46,151
Solving for shadow length gives four hundred cotangent theta.

417
00:29:47,271 --> 00:29:49,663
Differentiate the cotangent expression.

418
00:29:49,933 --> 00:29:55,633
Its negative derivative multiplies the negative angle rate, so the shadow length increases.

419
00:29:56,753 --> 00:30:01,223
At pi over six, sine is one half, so cosecant squared is four.

420
00:30:01,493 --> 00:30:04,790
The shadow length is increasing at four hundred feet per hour.

421
00:30:05,910 --> 00:30:09,544
A tangent line gives a simple estimate near a known input.

422
00:30:09,814 --> 00:30:12,937
Choose sixteen because its square root is exactly four.

423
00:30:13,207 --> 00:30:17,294
The approximation uses the function value and slope at that easy point.

424
00:30:18,414 --> 00:30:21,955
The square root derivative is one over two square root x.

425
00:30:22,225 --> 00:30:24,663
At sixteen, the slope is one eighth.

426
00:30:24,933 --> 00:30:29,508
This slope converts a small input change into an estimated output change.

427
00:30:30,628 --> 00:30:32,160
Build the tangent line.

428
00:30:32,430 --> 00:30:36,424
The change in x must be measured from sixteen, not from zero.

429
00:30:36,694 --> 00:30:41,907
This is an approximation to the square root function, rather than a new exact formula for it.

430
00:30:43,027 --> 00:30:46,034
The input increases by zero point zero one.

431
00:30:46,304 --> 00:30:49,055
Divide this change by eight and add four.

432
00:30:49,325 --> 00:30:52,553
Our estimate is four point zero zero one two five.

433
00:30:53,673 --> 00:30:58,654
The square root curve is concave down, so its tangent line lies above the curve.

434
00:30:58,924 --> 00:31:01,037
Our estimate is slightly too high.

435
00:31:01,307 --> 00:31:06,125
The actual value is about four point zero zero one two four nine eight.

436
00:31:06,395 --> 00:31:10,284
The approximation works well because the input is close to sixteen.

437
00:31:11,404 --> 00:31:15,073
Choose the easy nearby input two, not one point nine.

438
00:31:15,343 --> 00:31:20,416
The change from two to one point nine nine nine is negative zero point zero zero one.

439
00:31:21,536 --> 00:31:27,620
The tangent approximation uses the exact function value sixteen and the slope thirty two at the base point.

440
00:31:28,740 --> 00:31:32,536
Multiply slope by input change and add the base value.

441
00:31:32,806 --> 00:31:39,320
Because x to the fourth is concave up near two, the tangent estimate is slightly below the exact value.

442
00:31:40,440 --> 00:31:44,317
A differential is d y equals the derivative times d x.

443
00:31:44,587 --> 00:31:49,986
It gives the linearized output change, rather than the exact change in the function.

444
00:31:51,106 --> 00:31:56,133
Differentiate the polynomial, keeping the constant term derivative equal to zero.

445
00:31:57,253 --> 00:32:01,944
Evaluate the derivative at the given x before multiplying by the given d x.

446
00:32:02,214 --> 00:32:04,884
The requested differential is zero point eight.

447
00:32:06,004 --> 00:32:09,800
Use differentials to estimate how an edge measurement error propagates.

448
00:32:10,070 --> 00:32:14,331
We want the magnitude of the possible error, so use absolute values.

449
00:32:15,451 --> 00:32:19,677
The derivative of cube volume with respect to its edge is three a squared.

450
00:32:19,947 --> 00:32:26,226
Multiplying by the maximum edge error gives an estimated maximum volume error of two hundred seventy cubic

451
00:32:26,226 --> 00:32:26,936
centimeters.

452
00:32:28,056 --> 00:32:32,457
Relative error divides the estimated absolute error by the computed volume.

453
00:32:32,727 --> 00:32:35,873
It is zero point zero one, which is one percent.

454
00:32:36,143 --> 00:32:40,392
Differential estimates are first-order estimates, not exact error bounds.

455
00:32:41,512 --> 00:32:46,052
For part b, use the surface-area formula instead of the volume formula.

456
00:32:46,322 --> 00:32:48,040
The derivative is twelve a.

457
00:32:49,160 --> 00:32:53,885
The estimated maximum surface-area error is thirty six square centimeters.

458
00:32:54,155 --> 00:32:56,408
The units are area units this time.

459
00:32:57,528 --> 00:33:04,459
The relative area error is about zero point zero zero six six seven, or zero point six six seven percent.

460
00:33:04,729 --> 00:33:10,866
The relative volume error is three times the relative edge error; the relative area error is twice the relative

461
00:33:10,866 --> 00:33:11,474
edge error.

462
00:33:12,594 --> 00:33:13,929
Part a is false.

463
00:33:14,199 --> 00:33:17,206
E squared is a constant, not e to the x.

464
00:33:17,476 --> 00:33:20,425
Its derivative with respect to x is zero.

465
00:33:20,695 --> 00:33:24,956
The proposed answer two e would come from treating the constant e as a variable.

466
00:33:26,076 --> 00:33:27,539
Part b is false.

467
00:33:27,809 --> 00:33:33,463
X is in the exponent, so use the exponential rule, not the constant exponent power rule.

468
00:33:33,733 --> 00:33:37,634
The correct derivative is ten to the x times natural log ten.

469
00:33:38,754 --> 00:33:40,217
Part c is false.

470
00:33:40,487 --> 00:33:44,678
Natural log ten is another constant, so its derivative is zero.

471
00:33:44,948 --> 00:33:49,383
The formula one over x applies to natural log x when x varies.

472
00:33:50,503 --> 00:33:51,757
Part d is true.

473
00:33:52,027 --> 00:33:56,450
The polynomial has degree thirty, since the highest power is x to the thirtieth.

474
00:33:56,720 --> 00:33:59,251
Each derivative lowers its degree by one.

475
00:33:59,521 --> 00:34:02,726
After thirty one derivatives, every term is zero.

476
00:34:03,846 --> 00:34:07,770
Part e is the correct chain rule wherever f of x is positive.

477
00:34:08,040 --> 00:34:10,919
Under that usual domain assumption it is true.

478
00:34:11,189 --> 00:34:18,304
As literally stated with only differentiability of f, it needs a qualification: differentiability alone does not

479
00:34:18,304 --> 00:34:20,210
guarantee this formula exists.

480
00:34:21,330 --> 00:34:28,894
For example, f of x equals x squared is differentiable everywhere, but the square root of f is absolute x, which

481
00:34:28,894 --> 00:34:30,920
is not differentiable at zero.

482
00:34:31,190 --> 00:34:34,742
This explains why the positive-inside condition matters.

483
00:34:35,862 --> 00:34:37,360
Part f is false.

484
00:34:37,630 --> 00:34:43,424
The outside derivative must be evaluated at the inside input square root x, not at x.

485
00:34:44,544 --> 00:34:48,804
The correct formula has f prime of square root x in the numerator.

486
00:34:49,074 --> 00:34:54,566
If f of u is u squared, the original composition is x and its derivative is one.

487
00:34:54,836 --> 00:34:59,248
The incorrect formula gives square root x, which is not always one.

488
00:35:00,368 --> 00:35:01,656
Part g is true.

489
00:35:01,926 --> 00:35:05,839
This is the sum rule: differentiate each summand separately.

490
00:35:06,959 --> 00:35:08,364
Part h is false.

491
00:35:08,634 --> 00:35:13,324
The product rule is f prime times g, plus f times g prime.

492
00:35:13,594 --> 00:35:16,973
For a counterexample, let both functions equal x.

493
00:35:17,243 --> 00:35:23,071
The product is x squared and its derivative is two x, but multiplying the derivatives gives one.

494
00:35:24,191 --> 00:35:25,422
Part i is true.

495
00:35:25,692 --> 00:35:32,495
This is the chain rule, with the outside derivative evaluated at the unchanged inside function, then multiplied

496
00:35:32,495 --> 00:35:34,028
by the inside derivative.

497
00:35:35,148 --> 00:35:36,413
Part j is false.

498
00:35:36,683 --> 00:35:39,435
It uses the wrong input for f prime.

499
00:35:39,705 --> 00:35:43,675
Take f of u equal to u squared and g of x equal to x squared.

500
00:35:43,945 --> 00:35:50,590
The composition is x to the fourth, so its derivative is four x cubed, while the proposed expression is four x

501
00:35:50,590 --> 00:35:51,074
squared.

502
00:35:52,194 --> 00:35:56,907
We have now covered all fifty two numbered problems, including every lettered subpart.

503
00:35:57,177 --> 00:36:03,974
For a second pass, pause after each problem statement, solve it on paper, and use the next reveals to find the

504
00:36:03,974 --> 00:36:06,198
first point where your work differs.

505
00:36:07,318 --> 00:36:13,147
Your final checks are structure, chain-rule factors, signs, domain restrictions, and units.

506
00:36:13,417 --> 00:36:17,143
For motion, compare velocity and acceleration signs.

507
00:36:17,413 --> 00:36:21,430
For rates, differentiate before substituting changing values.

508
00:36:21,700 --> 00:36:26,449
For tangent lines and approximations, evaluate the slope at the given base point.
